@startuml
title Why compliance flags are delayed — and the window it opens

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 260

actor "Compliance officer\n(ENFORCEMENT_ROLE)" as Officer
database "Public state" as PS
actor "Target holder" as Target
participant "transfer\nexternal private" as Priv

== Why a plain public flag cannot be used ==

note over Priv, PS
  A private function is proved against a HISTORICAL state on the user's
  own device. It cannot read current mutable public state at all.
  Two ways out, and only one keeps the caller private:
  1. enqueue a public call to read the flag  ->  publishes the caller's
     address on every single transfer. Defeats the point of the token.
  2. make the flag a DelayedPublicMutable  ->  readable privately,
     because a scheduled change cannot take effect for a known window.
  This contract takes (2). The delay is the price of checking
  compliance state privately.
end note

== The freeze, and the window ==

Officer -> PS : freeze(target)
PS -> PS : schedule_value_change(frozen = true)
note right of PS
  The SCHEDULED change is visible in public state immediately.
  The EFFECTIVE value does not change until the delay elapses.
end note

group Window: roles_delay() — one hour initially, adjustable with set_roles_delay
  Target -> PS : reads the pending change — it is public
  Target -> Priv : transfer_private_to_private(...) — still succeeds
  Priv -> PS : is_frozen(target) still reads false
  note over Target, Priv
    The target can move funds until the delay elapses. This window
    is deterministic and cannot be closed by paying a higher fee,
    nor hidden by a private relay: it is protocol-enforced, not a
    consequence of mempool visibility.
    Mitigation is procedural: pause the token, schedule the freeze,
    wait out the delay, then unpause.
  end note
end

== After the delay ==

Target -> Priv : transfer_private_to_private(...)
Priv -> PS : is_frozen(target) now reads true
Priv --> Target : revert "Frozen: Sender"

note over Officer, Priv
  The same delay applies to every DelayedPublicMutable in the contract:
  the freeze flags, the blacklist and whitelist entries, the operations
  switch, and issuer_address.
  CHANGE_ROLES_DELAY_SECONDS = 3600 is the value at deployment. Since
  0.4.0 an admin may move it with set_roles_delay, bounded by
  MAX_ROLES_DELAY_SECONDS = 86400; an increase is immediate, a decrease
  takes effect after the difference.
end note

@enduml
